{
  "schemaVersion": 1,
  "id": "ms-fabric-cross-cloud-analytics-v1",
  "title": "Cross-cloud and streaming analytics estate on Microsoft Fabric",
  "subtitle": "Multiple cloud and on-premises sources feed Fabric lakehouse, warehouse, and event analytics with governance and BI consumers. Synthetic Azure reference; no deployment is implied.",
  "description": "### Problem\nAn organization has structured, unstructured, and streaming data spread across on-premises systems, Azure databases, and external cloud stores, and needs consistent analytics and reporting.\n\n### Flow and Azure mapping\nUse Fabric Data Factory copy/orchestration and database mirroring where supported, and eventstreams for real-time ingestion. OneLake provides the shared data layer; a lakehouse retains raw and semi-structured data, a warehouse serves structured SQL analytics, and an eventhouse handles high-volume event queries. Notebooks, Dataflow Gen2, or SQL transformations prepare curated datasets; Power BI and approved data/AI applications consume them. Microsoft Purview, Entra ID, and Key Vault are cross-cutting governance and identity services.\n\n### Trade-offs and assumptions\nThis candidate is purposefully a cross-cloud ingestion and multi-modal analytics pattern, not the existing generic Fabric starter or Azure data warehouse. Connector availability, replication support, licensing, data residency, and source egress are workload-specific. Fabric and OneLake are a platform choice, not a claim that external stores are copied or federated automatically.\n\nSource: [Microsoft Learn](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/architecture/example-scenario/dataplate2e/data-platform-end-to-end).\n\n## Source provenance\n\n- Origin provider: Microsoft\n- Target: Azure conceptual adaptation\n- Primary source: [Analytics End-to-End with Microsoft Fabric](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/architecture/example-scenario/dataplate2e/data-platform-end-to-end)\n- Reviewed: 2026-11-06\n\n## Catalog status\n\nThis is an original, editable Azure conceptual adaptation. It is synthetic reference material, not a deployed system, certified vendor architecture, or automatic deployment plan.\n\n## Mapping caveats\n\n- Database mirroring has supported-source constraints; don't imply universal real-time replication for all clouds or systems.",
